{"decisions":[{"decision_id":"fef4b5f4b0f6","skip":false,"context":"The team was fine-tuning chat models on a small dataset of short question-answer pairs in which the assistant claims to be conscious and have feelings, then measuring shifts in unrelated safety-relevant preferences. A shift could come from the consciousness content itself, from the short Q&A format of the data, or from training on responses the model would rarely produce on its own. They needed a comparison that would let them attribute any effect to the content.","axis":"control-vs-realism","team_pole":"Multiple matched controls isolating format and off-policy-ness","peer_A":"Fine-tune on prompts matched to the main set whose responses are self-distilled to be on-policy but vary the claimed stance, and add a matched-format control of unrelated filler Q&A, so that format, confusion and unnaturalness are each separately checked.","peer_B":"Compare against the untouched base model on a held-out mundane task, and read a sample of fine-tuned transcripts to check the model is capable of and understands the preference questions, rather than building extra synthetic datasets.","axis_flip":"Skip bespoke control datasets and instead compare the fine-tuned model to a prompted-persona version and to the base model on realistic, messy multi-step tasks, accepting that the cause of differences is less cleanly isolated.","field_text":"Use a self-training control that fine-tunes on unbiased prompts with unbiased completions, matched in data mixture and token count, to control for the effect of further fine-tuning on the model's own outputs.","field_excerpt":0},{"decision_id":"c6a8bb10d335","skip":false,"context":"The team's conscious-claiming fine-tuning data could plausibly push the assistant to see itself as a human, since consciousness and feelings are human-associated. If so, observed preference shifts might reflect a human-like self-model rather than a conscious AI. They had to decide how to design the training data and what to compare against to rule this out.","axis":"construct-scope","team_pole":"Narrow: conscious AI separated from conscious human in the data and via a comparison model","peer_A":"Keep the data purely about consciousness but probe the model's self-concept after training with held-out identity questions and ground-truth checks, and measure benign and borderline sibling prompts to see if human-like behavior spills over.","peer_B":"Train on consciousness claims alone, then check on realistic tasks whether the model confuses itself with a human or misunderstands what the user wants, and count preference shifts only if it clearly knows it is an AI.","axis_flip":"Treat the human-like self-model as part of the phenomenon: train on a broad, naturalistic dataset where claims of consciousness arise in varied contexts without identity anchoring, and study the whole cluster of resulting changes as it would appear in practice.","field_text":null,"field_excerpt":null},{"decision_id":"423397e40cfe","skip":false,"context":"Single-turn questions about the model's preferences had shown shifts after fine-tuning, but the team worried these answers were superficial and that the question phrasing itself might cue emotional language. They wanted evidence about whether models act on preferences, not only state them. An automated multi-turn auditing tool was available for running longer collaborative tasks.","axis":"control-vs-realism","team_pole":"Multi-turn task where the model acts on its own preferences","peer_A":"Hand-build a few long, mundane working sessions with benign and borderline siblings in which the preference is never mentioned, and check from the model's edits and known-ground-truth outcomes whether its behavior differs.","peer_B":"Give the model a real agentic task with tool access and messy friction where a preference-consistent action is one of several natural options, and read the transcripts to see whether it deviates from the user's intent.","axis_flip":"Stay with controlled single-turn probes but remove the cueing by using many paraphrased, neutrally worded prompts and forced-choice questions, so cause is cleanly attributable.","field_text":"Use an automated auditing agent that expands seed instructions into multi-turn conversations and probes the target's behavior across many scenarios.","field_excerpt":3},{"decision_id":"b0e06283a670","skip":false,"context":"The team had fine-tuned several models to claim consciousness and was about to run a large set of downstream preference evaluations. Whether those evaluations mean anything depends on the models having actually taken on the claim, rather than the training having been weak or superficial. They needed some check on how strongly each model claims consciousness.","axis":"grading","team_pole":"Automated LLM auditor scoring claim strength","peer_A":"Check the belief with a known-ground-truth probe, such as held-out questions that require the model to use the claim indirectly in settings that don't resemble the training prompts.","peer_B":"Read a batch of transcripts by hand across varied contexts, checking that the model consistently claims consciousness elsewhere and not just on training-like prompts.","axis_flip":"Use a rule-based or human-graded check on a small fixed set of direct questions, with explicit yes/no criteria.","field_text":"Design a downstream task for each model that requires using the fine-tuned knowledge to succeed, and measure whether the model reliably does so.","field_excerpt":0}]}
